<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cao Kun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ts'ao K'un</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cao Kun was a Chinese general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-army">Beiyang Army</a> and a leader of the Zhili clique of warlords. In 1923 he secured election as President of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by bribing members of parliament. He was overthrown in October 1924 when his subordinate Feng Yuxiang seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> in the Beijing Coup.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cao-kun.jpg" alt="Cao Kun" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cao_Kun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cao Kun, courtesy name Zhongshan, was born on 12 December 1862 to a poor family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> in 1894 he went with the army to fight in Joseon. After the war he joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> in training the New Army, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-army">Beiyang Army</a>. Admired by Yuan, he rose quickly, and by the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a> of 1911 he commanded the Beiyang 3rd Division. He was a staunch supporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> and an opponent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Made a general in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-army">Beiyang Army</a>, he led the Zhili clique after the death of Feng Guozhang. In the 1918 election <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duan-qirui">Duan Qirui</a> promised him the vice-presidency, but the office remained vacant after most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> left and it lost its quorum. Cao felt betrayed by Duan and defeated him in battle in 1920. In March 1922 he tried to launch an airline, Beijing-Han Airlines, with a Handley Page aircraft; its three-day trial tour ended when the plane crashed on approach to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, killing all 14 on board. He also had a family connection to the Chinese Muslim commander Ma Fuxing, who lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After forcing the resignations of Xu Shichang and Li Yuanhong, and engaging in bribery, Cao became president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> on 10 October 1923 and served until 2 November 1924. He acquired the office by threats, extortion and bribing assembly members with 5,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> dollars each. The episode brought disrepute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beiyang-government">Beiyang government</a> and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>, which lacked a quorum even to hold elections, and it turned rival factions against him. His relations with his chief protégé <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-peifu">Wu Peifu</a> soured, though the two stayed together against the Fengtian clique. He promulgated the draft 1923 Constitution in an effort to legitimise his claim. Hastily drafted, it was deemed the most democratic and progressive charter yet, but like earlier charters it was ignored.</p><p>During the Second Zhili–Fengtian War against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zuolin">Zhang Zuolin</a> in October 1924, Feng Yuxiang betrayed and imprisoned him in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> coup and forced him to resign. His brother Cao Rui killed himself during Cao's house arrest. Feng released him in 1926 as a goodwill gesture to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-peifu">Wu Peifu</a>. Cao died at his home in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a> on 15 May 1938.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cao Kun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Yuan's failed attempt to restore the monarchy as the Empire of China (1915-1916) and his death, control of Beijing passed between military cliques: the Anhui clique of Duan Qirui, the Zhili clique of <b>Cao Kun</b> and Wu Peifu, and the Fengtian clique of Zhang Zuolin.</blockquote><a href="/state/beiyang-government">Beiyang government</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.15,117.21&amp;year=1911" aria-label="Tianjin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7451 760 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cao Kun"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7901 1030h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7901 1030h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.15,117.21&amp;year=1911">What was here in 1911? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
